WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:03.129
SCP-3930 does not exist…

00:00:03.129 --> 00:00:04.330
Thanks for watching.

00:00:04.330 --> 00:00:06.210
Now check out “SCP-

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:10.460
The following information is only known to
seven living people.

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:14.960
Even those actively guarding this anomaly
will never know this information.

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:20.890
Every single person who believes they are
aware of the existence of SCP-3930 has the

00:00:20.890 --> 00:00:23.830
potential to increase its danger.

00:00:23.830 --> 00:00:30.760
SCP-3930 has been given the risk class “Critical”
- the highest possible risk class for an anomaly,

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:36.810
meaning that individuals nearby will invariably
be exposed to acute, life-threatening harm,

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:39.820
and that these effects cannot be mitigated.

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:44.980
You learn about SCP-3930 at your own risk,
with the knowledge that this awareness puts

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:46.900
your life at hazard.

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:52.030
Someday soon, you may be forced to make the
ultimate sacrifice for the good of all life

00:00:52.030 --> 00:00:53.720
and matter in the universe.

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:58.850
That is why it is imperative that you know
one essential thing...

00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:02.070
SCP-3930 does not exist.

00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:06.280
Now with that out of the way, we only have
one question for you...

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:07.990
Can you hear the screaming?

00:01:07.990 --> 00:01:12.950
It was when Dr. Vasiliev heard the screaming
that he and his ragtag team of scientists

00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:15.070
knew it was already over.

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:20.830
The year was 1971, and Dr. Vasiliev’s team
was stationed at a highly secretive Soviet

00:01:20.830 --> 00:01:26.280
research outpost near what would later be
established as the Russian town of Usinsk,

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:28.100
within the Komi Republic.

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:32.049
As far as Dr. Vasiliev was concerned, they
were dead men already.

00:01:32.049 --> 00:01:35.270
Well, that’s not entirely accurate.

00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:40.369
To be dead is to be… something, and the
men that had once been stationed with them

00:01:40.369 --> 00:01:44.720
but were here no longer could not be considered
“something” at all.

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:50.310
Their brush with the immensely powerful and
classified anomaly later known as SCP-3930

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:52.729
had robbed them of their very existence.

00:01:52.729 --> 00:01:58.880
Now, Dr. Vasiliev knew that he and the few
remaining men stationed at the base with him

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:00.250
would be next.

00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:04.810
The Soviet government had dispatched a detachment
consisting of around a hundred soldiers and

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:08.269
scientists to investigate the anomaly in the
Komi Republic.

00:02:08.269 --> 00:02:12.739
As far as they could tell, it appeared to
occupy a one-kilometer squared area within

00:02:12.739 --> 00:02:17.840
a forest, but to say that it really “occupied”
anything would be a stretch.

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:24.170
And out of the over hundred men who had been
dispatched, now scarcely thirty remained.

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:28.090
Observation teams had been sent into the anomalous
zone to collect data on what exactly they

00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:32.860
were dealing with, but each time, it seemed
as though the researchers were doomed to know

00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:36.650
less and less about what they were investigating.

00:02:36.650 --> 00:02:42.519
From a distance, the area didn’t even appear
to be anomalous: It looked simply like a continuation

00:02:42.519 --> 00:02:47.879
of the forest, complete with the expected
flora, fauna, and even some distant evidence

00:02:47.879 --> 00:02:51.269
of human habitation, like an abandoned building.

00:02:51.269 --> 00:02:56.309
But without fail, nobody who had ever seen
the building up close had ever returned.

00:02:56.309 --> 00:03:03.340
Thanks to SCP-3930, they had simply ceased
to be - because, simply put, when you introduce

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:07.110
something into nothing, the “nothing”
wins.

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:14.030
Dr. Vasiliev had seen too many good men disappear
into oblivion, and soon, it would be his turn,

00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:15.030
too.

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:18.549
But first, something horrible would crawl
out of the zone.

00:03:18.549 --> 00:03:21.150
Something that shouldn’t exist.

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:24.620
Something that didn’t exist.

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:27.489
Something that never will exist.

00:03:27.489 --> 00:03:29.579
And yet here it was.

00:03:29.579 --> 00:03:33.299
In science, the observer effect is a dreaded
phenomenon.

00:03:33.299 --> 00:03:37.610
All experiments involve the observation of
phenomena to collect data and then prove or

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:39.779
disprove a hypothesis.

00:03:39.779 --> 00:03:44.430
But the observer effect describes the frustrating
scenario where the mere act of observing a

00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:48.450
phenomenon alters it, reducing the usefulness
of the data.

00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:53.419
A common example of this in mundane life is
checking the pressure of your car or bike’s

00:03:53.419 --> 00:03:57.730
tires - to do this, you need to let out some
air, thus changing the pressure.

00:03:57.730 --> 00:04:01.680
But pressure holds a very different meaning
when you’re working with what might be the

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:04.680
universe’s largest patch of nothing.

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:09.290
Because of their prime directive - namely,
researching the anomaly - Dr. Vasiliev and

00:04:09.290 --> 00:04:16.940
his fellow scientists were effectively forced
to observe SCP-3930, and 3930 apparently doesn’t

00:04:16.940 --> 00:04:17.940
like that.

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:22.850
The scientists only noticed sounds at first;
barely perceptible little screeches that could

00:04:22.850 --> 00:04:26.340
easily be written off as your mind playing
a trick on you.

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:28.950
And, in a sense, it is.

00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:35.980
But around SCP-3930, the tricks played by
the mind become a lot more… practical, than

00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:37.320
anywhere else.

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:42.370
What started as sounds soon came to take on
a visual form, as more people became aware

00:04:42.370 --> 00:04:46.840
of the anomaly and made futile attempts to
observe it from the outside.

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:51.820
These scattered visual and audio forms soon
became nicknamed “Screamers”, for their

00:04:51.820 --> 00:04:53.460
horrific, hateful wailing.

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:59.260
Dr. Vasiliev and his team believed that, despite
the extremely hostile nature of the anomalies,

00:04:59.260 --> 00:05:03.190
they had no way of ever leaving the anomalous
zone.

00:05:03.190 --> 00:05:09.320
That belief was soon proved wrong, and not
long after, only ten researchers remained.

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:14.070
It seemed that the last of Dr. Vasiliev’s
hope died with the lost men but there was

00:05:14.070 --> 00:05:16.150
some good news at least.

00:05:16.150 --> 00:05:21.820
With only ten left to observe the SCP-3930
anomaly - the Screamers began to disappear

00:05:21.820 --> 00:05:25.220
and the anomalous zone became stable once
more.

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:31.880
Ten, it seemed, was the upper limit of observers
that could distantly engage with SCP-3930

00:05:31.880 --> 00:05:34.320
before all hell broke loose.

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:37.060
And then the SCP Foundation arrived.

00:05:37.060 --> 00:05:42.340
By the time they reached the long-since-defunct
research outpost, Dr. Vasiliev was the only

00:05:42.340 --> 00:05:43.500
one left.

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:48.400
The final members of his team had sacrificed
themselves by walking into the void and joining

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:50.050
non-existence.

00:05:50.050 --> 00:05:55.961
Vasiliev himself remained for only one purpose:
To tell the Foundation what he knew, what

00:05:55.961 --> 00:06:02.940
they needed to know, before bowing out into
SCP-3930 himself - along with however many

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:08.640
of the Foundation personnel needed to go to
keep the number of active observers at a nice,

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:10.280
clean ten.

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:12.420
That is the burden of knowing.

00:06:12.420 --> 00:06:18.000
And accepting that burden is to accept that
you may one day need to become unknown in

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:22.740
order to protect your fellow human beings
from the horrors that lay within.

00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:28.240
But what is SCP-3930, and what are these so-called
Screamers that lurk within?

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:30.660
We have the answers to these questions.

00:06:30.660 --> 00:06:35.500
The only question for you is whether you’re
ready to take on the grave responsibility

00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:37.410
of knowing too.

00:06:37.410 --> 00:06:40.750
SCP-3930 does not exist.

00:06:40.750 --> 00:06:45.530
That isn’t just a cover-up by the O5 Council
- it doesn’t “not exist” in the official

00:06:45.530 --> 00:06:51.150
capacity, it is quite literally the world’s
largest patch of nothingness, and anything

00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:54.410
that enters it is doomed to become nothing
as well.

00:06:54.410 --> 00:06:59.240
It’s a difficult concept to grasp because
fundamentally, all language was built as a

00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:07.660
tool to communicate concepts that, on some
level, exist, and SCP-3930 does not qualify.

00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:13.080
Close your eyes for a moment - seriously,
do it - and try to imagine nothing.

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:14.240
What do you see?

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:16.480
An infinite space of pure darkness?

00:07:16.480 --> 00:07:19.050
A white void, stretching on into infinity?

00:07:19.050 --> 00:07:23.800
We’re sorry to inform you that neither of
these counts as being truly nothing.

00:07:23.800 --> 00:07:29.540
Though darkness may be the absence of light,
to see that blackness is to still see something.

00:07:29.540 --> 00:07:32.520
The darkness even implies the existence of
light.

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:37.090
The same can be said for the white void - for
such a thing to exist, we need both the concept

00:07:37.090 --> 00:07:40.770
of the color white and the idea of a void.

00:07:40.770 --> 00:07:47.540
These are perhaps the lowest bars for “existence”
imaginable, but SCP-3930 doesn’t meet them.

00:07:47.540 --> 00:07:50.680
It is well and truly nothing.

00:07:50.680 --> 00:07:51.770
Nothing at all.

00:07:51.770 --> 00:07:57.030
And because it is nothing, its containment
procedures are largely just a matter of information

00:07:57.030 --> 00:08:01.940
control - if nobody knows about it, the threat
is contained.

00:08:01.940 --> 00:08:06.470
If ever someone just happened to wander in,
they would stop existing, so the problem would

00:08:06.470 --> 00:08:10.300
take care of itself, as callous as that sounds.

00:08:10.300 --> 00:08:15.270
On its own, the anomaly would technically
be harmless, but when mixed with humans, things

00:08:15.270 --> 00:08:18.170
become a whole lot more complicated.

00:08:18.170 --> 00:08:23.360
You see, unless you have the misfortune to
run into SCP-3930, you will spend your life

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:26.910
without ever having to encounter true nothingness.

00:08:26.910 --> 00:08:31.650
Even when you die, the matter that comprises
your body and the energy that comprises your

00:08:31.650 --> 00:08:37.559
consciousness will remain, they will just
be transferred into other forms.

00:08:37.559 --> 00:08:43.289
Humans have no reason to ever encounter nothingness,
and thus, we have no physiological or psychological

00:08:43.289 --> 00:08:47.480
equipment to truly observe and comprehend
nothingness.

00:08:47.480 --> 00:08:51.709
When we’re given a true blank, our instinct
is to fill it.

00:08:51.709 --> 00:08:57.290
And that right there is the true danger of
SCP-3930, and the very reason we have to deal

00:08:57.290 --> 00:08:58.649
with Screamers.

00:08:58.649 --> 00:09:01.990
Or, as you may know them, Pattern Screamers.

00:09:01.990 --> 00:09:08.459
That’s right - if this whole setup seemed
awfully familiar to you, it’s because SCP-3930

00:09:08.459 --> 00:09:13.819
is the absolute global epicenter of Pattern
Screamers, the hateful entities we’ve encountered

00:09:13.819 --> 00:09:18.089
before in anomalies like SCP-000.

00:09:18.089 --> 00:09:23.240
Nothingness is the natural environment of
Pattern Screamers, but because SCP-3930 is

00:09:23.240 --> 00:09:28.600
the only place where true nothingness exists
on our layer of reality, it’s also the only

00:09:28.600 --> 00:09:34.080
place where Pattern Screamers are able to
directly interact with humans if they become

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:39.850
powerful and defined enough - often to deadly
and terrifying results.

00:09:39.850 --> 00:09:44.279
Pattern Screamers are an incredibly difficult
concept to wrap your head around sometimes,

00:09:44.279 --> 00:09:46.129
but allow us to explain.

00:09:46.129 --> 00:09:49.529
Close your eyes again, and this time, picture
a clown.

00:09:49.529 --> 00:09:54.139
Not a specific clown like Ronald McDonald
or Pennywise, you have to make up your own

00:09:54.139 --> 00:09:55.139
clown.

00:09:55.139 --> 00:10:00.410
Let’s say he’s got green hair, a big,
blue rubber nose, a wide smile, and polka

00:10:00.410 --> 00:10:02.040
dot clown shoes.

00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:07.360
The other details are up to you - go as simple
or as complicated as you like, as long as

00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:10.899
it’s an entirely new clown you’ve never
seen in real life.

00:10:10.899 --> 00:10:16.160
Now hold the image in your head, really focus
on it, you can even use your imagination to

00:10:16.160 --> 00:10:18.230
make him dance or walk around.

00:10:18.230 --> 00:10:21.499
Fundamentally, this clown does not exist.

00:10:21.499 --> 00:10:27.000
Aside from being the product of chemical and
electrical processes in your brain, if this

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:33.160
is indeed an entirely new clown in your mind,
it exists only on the level of thought.

00:10:33.160 --> 00:10:37.180
Now imagine if this clown in your mind was
somehow aware of the fact it wasn’t real,

00:10:37.180 --> 00:10:40.970
and that it was only the product of your imagination.

00:10:40.970 --> 00:10:46.680
Imagine that this clown is infuriated by that
fact - it despises you for bringing it into

00:10:46.680 --> 00:10:51.889
being, doomed to be a concept straddling existence
and non-existence.

00:10:51.889 --> 00:10:54.410
Imagine this clown wants to hurt you.

00:10:54.410 --> 00:11:02.149
It really, really, really wants to hurt you,
with every fiber of its non-existent being.

00:11:02.149 --> 00:11:04.639
That right there is a Pattern Screamer.

00:11:04.639 --> 00:11:10.129
And the frightening thing about SCP-3930 is
that, if enough people observe it directly,

00:11:10.129 --> 00:11:13.230
these Pattern Screamers can become real.

00:11:13.230 --> 00:11:18.269
They can crawl out of nothing, and then, they
can show you exactly how they feel about you

00:11:18.269 --> 00:11:20.730
bringing them into existence.

00:11:20.730 --> 00:11:26.040
And when they’re done, you’ll probably
regret ever existing in the first place.

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:33.560
Think of them as hostile, sentient hallucinations,
capable of reaching maximum power around SCP-3930.

00:11:33.560 --> 00:11:38.399
This is because, when humans are given a lack
of stimulus, their minds simply create their

00:11:38.399 --> 00:11:39.399
own.

00:11:39.399 --> 00:11:43.369
This is why people believe they see the forest
simply continuing into the anomalous zone

00:11:43.369 --> 00:11:47.410
- it’s the only way the mind can comprehend
what is going on here.

00:11:47.410 --> 00:11:53.269
It’s like the phenomenon of Pareidolia - humans
have a natural tendency to see patterns in

00:11:53.269 --> 00:11:55.480
things, even if they were never really there.

00:11:55.480 --> 00:12:00.779
It’s from these very patterns that the Pattern
Screamers gain both their form and their name.

00:12:00.779 --> 00:12:06.490
They’re monsters that we create, cursed
to live hellish half-existences, and they

00:12:06.490 --> 00:12:07.959
hate us for it.

00:12:07.959 --> 00:12:13.769
Dr. Vasiliev described SCP-3930 as a kind
of “hateful mirror” that reflects back

00:12:13.769 --> 00:12:19.190
the ugliest shards of our own minds, first
broken in the futile quest to comprehend the

00:12:19.190 --> 00:12:21.069
true nature of nothingness.

00:12:21.069 --> 00:12:27.639
Thousands have likely been lost inside SCP-3930
- if we can even call them “lost”, as

00:12:27.639 --> 00:12:33.519
lost implies that something is still out there,
somewhere, even if we never actually find

00:12:33.519 --> 00:12:34.519
it.

00:12:34.519 --> 00:12:37.959
This cannot be said for the victims of SCP-3930.

00:12:37.959 --> 00:12:40.480
They are nowhere and nothing.

00:12:40.480 --> 00:12:44.380
They can’t be found again because there
is nothing to find.

00:12:44.380 --> 00:12:50.399
The Foundation has used D-Class personnel
to perform manned recon missions into SCP-3930,

00:12:50.399 --> 00:12:54.699
feeding information back to the few members
of mission control permitted to be aware of

00:12:54.699 --> 00:12:58.360
SCP-3930’s existence without immediate termination.

00:12:58.360 --> 00:13:03.410
The D-Classes have ventured well into the
heart of nothingness, even reaching and entering

00:13:03.410 --> 00:13:05.829
the building that can be seen in the distance.

00:13:05.829 --> 00:13:09.480
There, they’ve found messes of haunting
inconsistencies.

00:13:09.480 --> 00:13:14.149
The place looks like it’s been abandoned
for decades, but also has evidence of recent

00:13:14.149 --> 00:13:15.269
habitation.

00:13:15.269 --> 00:13:17.279
Doors and windows move.

00:13:17.279 --> 00:13:19.629
Curtains and hallways go on forever.

00:13:19.629 --> 00:13:23.980
They feel a sound in their ears that’s similar
to the screech of TV static.

00:13:23.980 --> 00:13:28.379
No, not a screech, really more of a scream.

00:13:28.379 --> 00:13:35.910
It’s always in that moment that the realization
hits them: They don’t exist, not anymore,

00:13:35.910 --> 00:13:37.339
and they haven’t for a little while now.

00:13:37.339 --> 00:13:42.589
They’re just a few scattered memories of
the Foundation researcher talking to them,

00:13:42.589 --> 00:13:46.529
reflected back by SCP-3930’s hateful mirror.

00:13:46.529 --> 00:13:51.459
And moments later, they’re gone, almost
like they were never here at all.

00:13:51.459 --> 00:13:55.300
So if ever anyone asks you, “What is SCP-3930?”

00:13:55.300 --> 00:14:00.459
You know that there’s only ever one answer.

00:14:00.459 --> 00:14:02.589
SCP-3930 does not exist.

00:14:02.589 --> 00:14:09.399
Now check out “The Lost SCP - SCP-000”
and “SCP-096 - Look at a Picture of Shy

00:14:09.399 --> 00:14:10.420
Guy in Space?

00:14:10.420 --> 00:14:15.819
The Shy Guy Questions and Theories” to delve
into more of the SCP Foundation’s deepest,

00:14:15.819 --> 00:14:16.619
darkest secrets!

